# Silo — SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for every version number in the project.
#
# Edit a version here, then regenerate the committed Swift mirror:
#   Scripts/gen-versions.sh        # writes Sources/SiloKit/Versions.swift
# (Scripts/build-app.sh runs it for you.) Nothing else in the repo should hard-code a version.
#
# Shell-sourceable (KEY=VALUE, no spaces) so every script AND the Swift codegen read the exact same values.

# --- Silo app ---
SILO_VERSION=0.4.7
# GitHub repo (owner/name). Hosts BOTH the app releases the updater checks AND the wine-cx-* runtime
# builds the Wine tab installs.
SILO_GITHUB_REPO=mikaelhug/Silo

# --- Wine runtime (built from CrossOver's FOSS source in CI) ---
# The CrossOver source version that Scripts/build-wine.sh / .github/workflows/build-wine.yml compile from
# (crossover-sources-<ver>.tar.gz at media.codeweavers.com/pub/crossover/source/). The app downloads the
# LATEST published wine-cx-* release at runtime, so this pins the BUILD input, not what the app loads.
# (CI's wine-autoupdate.yml may bump past this automatically when CrossOver ships a newer source.)
CROSSOVER_VERSION=26.2.0

# --- SDL (winebus's game-controller backend) ---
# Wine's winebus.sys `dlopen`s libSDL2 for its SDL controller backend (which, with Wine's default
# `Map Controllers=1`, remaps ANY recognized pad to a standard XInput gamepad — the "controllers just
# work" behaviour). Scripts/build-wine.sh / build-wine.yml build this EXACT SDL from libsdl-org source
# (x86_64, to match the x86_64 Wine) and bundle libSDL2-2.0.0.dylib into the runtime. Pinned to the
# version CrossOver 26 ships (2.30.12): winebus's SDL-init code is identical (same CrossOver source), so
# mirroring CrossOver's dylib is what avoids the off-main-thread NSWindow abort a generic (Homebrew)
# libSDL2 caused. SDL is zlib-licensed (freely redistributable) and is NOT Wine, so constraint #8 is
# unaffected. Build-input only — the app never downloads SDL separately, so this is NOT mirrored into
# Versions.swift.
SDL_VERSION=2.30.12

# --- DXMT runtime (the optional older-games D3D10/11 backend) ---
# 3Shain's DXMT is the Metal translation layer Silo overlays as the GPTK fallback. Scripts/build-dxmt.sh
# / .github/workflows/build-dxmt.yml build it from this upstream tag against the CrossOver Wine above.
# Pinned to the EXACT version CrossOver 26 bundles, so DXMT ↔ Wine is the pairing CrossOver itself ships
# (CrossOver vendors DXMT from this same upstream — building it here keeps the canonical, reproducible
# build incl. its git submodules). DXMT is NOT Wine, so constraint #8 (Wine = CrossOver-FOSS only) is
# unaffected. LLVM_MINGW_VERSION is the cross-toolchain (mstorsjo/llvm-mingw) DXMT's cross-file pins.
DXMT_REPO=3Shain/dxmt
DXMT_VERSION=v0.72
LLVM_MINGW_VERSION=20231017

# --- DXVK runtime (the DirectX 9 + broad-compat Vulkan backend) ---
# DXVK (doitsujin/dxvk, zlib) is Silo's DirectX 9 translator AND the compatibility fallback for the D3D10/11
# titles the Metal backends can't drive: D3D9/10/11 → Vulkan → the wine runtime's already-bundled MoltenVK →
# Metal. Built STOCK from upstream (no patches) and run NATIVE — its dlls are seeded into the game prefix and
# overridden `=n` (Scripts/build-dxvk.sh / .github/workflows/build-dxvk.yml cross-compile it with meson +
# llvm-mingw for both ABIs). DXVK is NOT Wine, so constraint #8 is unaffected; never a third-party prebuilt.
# It reuses LLVM_MINGW_VERSION above (same cross toolchain as DXMT). The DXVK dlls themselves are a pure
# Windows-PE cross-compile (no Metal toolchain), but the MoltenVK below IS built from source and DOES need
# full Xcode — so a Command-Line-Tools-only box can build the dlls while build-dxvk.sh skips MoltenVK (and
# warns): that artifact is NOT usable on its own. Build the shippable artifact in CI (build-dxvk.yml).
# ✅ Phase 0 on-device result (2026-08-04, Apple M4 Pro): **DXVK v1.10.3 creates a real D3D11 device at feature
# level 11_0** through Silo's own from-source wine → winevulkan → MoltenVK → Metal. v2.6.2 was NOT validated
# (it was only tested against the stock MoltenVK below, which fails for every DXVK version) — re-test before
# bumping: a newer DXVK needs more Vulkan features MoltenVK may lack.
DXVK_REPO=doitsujin/dxvk
DXVK_VERSION=v1.10.3

# --- MoltenVK (the Vulkan→Metal driver DXVK runs on) ---
# ⚠️ LOAD-BEARING, and settled empirically on 2026-08-04 (Apple M4 Pro, D3D11CreateDevice probe):
#   • STOCK Khronos MoltenVK CANNOT create a D3D device for DXVK at ANY feature level — not 1.4.1 (Homebrew,
#     driver 0.2.2209) and NOT 1.2.11 built from Khronos source in our own CI. Both fail down to 9_1.
#   • CodeWeavers' PATCHED MoltenVK succeeds immediately at feature level 11_0.
#   • The measured difference is `VK_EXT_transform_feedback`, which DXVK requires for D3D10/11 stream-out.
#     Stock MoltenVK does not implement it; CodeWeavers' does.
# ✅ VALIDATED END-TO-END 2026-08-04: a CI-built artifact using this source reaches **feature level 11_0**
# (`D3D11CreateDevice` probe, Apple M4 Pro, MoltenVK 1.2.10 / Vulkan 1.2.290) — identical to CrossOver's own
# binary, where every stock build failed at every level.
#
# So MoltenVK is built from **CodeWeavers' published source** — `sources/moltenvk` inside the very
# `crossover-sources-<CROSSOVER_VERSION>.tar.gz` that Scripts/build-wine.sh already downloads (its
# MVKExtensions.def declares EXT_transform_feedback; upstream's does not). No version pin of our own: it
# tracks CROSSOVER_VERSION, so MoltenVK stays paired with the Wine it ships beside — exactly the DXMT↔Wine
# rule, and the same single trusted source constraint #8 names for Wine. We deliberately do NOT maintain a
# private MoltenVK patch set: the upstream that works is already published.
